          %p
            For the second criterium, consider the complete quadrangle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mi V
                    %mi W
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O V W U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            are two out of 3 diagonal points, the third pair of the mutually opposite sides in the criterium are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O W
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V U
            (their intersection is the third diagonal point).
          %p
            Now, one has to prove that if one takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C
            distinct points that there is a unique
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            with above properties, that is the construction does not depend on the choice of the points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            (or equivalently,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U,V
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U,V,C
            colinear (or equivalently, the choice of points
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U, O
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            in the construction. This however uses the fact that the plane is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Desarguesian"} Desarguesian
          %h3#a_degenerate_case_of_6_intersection_points_of_a_quadrangle A degenerate case of 6 intersection points of a quadrangle
          %p
            Given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+quadrangle"} complete quadrangle
            and a line in the same projective plane, one can consider the 6 points of intersection of a line with sides of quadrangle and name it in this order: first 3 points, say
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E,F,E
            are intersections of the 3 remaining sides opposite to the first 3 sides in the same order. The fact that the 6 points are obtained this way is denoted by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mi B
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mi E
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(A B C, D E F)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C,D,E
            are fixed than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            can be determined uniquely (it does not depend on a choice of the quadrangle giving the first 5 points). There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+involution"} projective involution
            sending each of the 6 intersections of the line with a side of the quadrangle to the intersection of the line with the opposite side (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pappus%27+involution+theorem"}> Pappus' involution theorem
            ).
          %p
            If the line on which the 6-tuple is standing is one of the diagonals of the quadrangle, then two pairs of points coincide by the definition of the diagonal (e.g.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=D
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = E
          %h2#harmonic_pencil Harmonic pencil
          %p Dually, we can consider a pencil of lines through a fixed point. 4 lines in this pencil are in a harmonic ratio if they by duality correspond to a harmonic 4-tuple of points in the dual projective plane of lines, but it can be characterized in many different ways including that the intersections of the 4 lines with one, and then automatically any other line not in the pencil are a harmonic 4-tuple of points. Thus to be harmonic is also an invariant under perspectivities.
          %h2#literature Literature
          %p Modern treatment is in Chapter 6 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcel+Berger"}> Marcel Berger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Géométrie
          %p A synthetic treatment is for example in
          %ul
            %li#Palman1984
              Dominik Palman,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Projektivna geometrija
